#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.12"
# dependencies = ["httpx==0.28.1", "rich==15.0.0", "typer==0.27.2"]
# ///
#MISE description="[human] Reconcile eblume's forge desired state — the agents + horkos-forge bots' collaborations, webhooks, and labels (repos.json) and branch protections (forge/branch-protections.json); --check is the single forge drift check (weekly + same-repo PRs)"
#MISE alias=["ara","agent-repo-access"]
#USAGE flag "--dry-run" help="Show what would change without changing it"
#USAGE flag "--check" help="Like --dry-run, but exit 1 if anything is out of sync (for CI on PRs)"
#USAGE flag "--token <token>" help="Forgejo API token; the caller must be admin on the target repos (default: 1Password)"
#USAGE flag "--skip-if-no-token" help="Exit 0 instead of failing when $FORGE_REPO_WRITE_TOKEN is unset (CI bootstrap)"
#USAGE flag "--pr-base-ref <ref>" help="PR mode: also report drift vs the base ref — intended drift warns, unexpected drift fails (CI on same-repo PRs)"
#USAGE flag "--report-file <path>" help="Write the PR-check intended-drift report to this file (CI posts it as a PR comment)"
"""Reconcile which of eblume's repos the `agents` and `horkos-forge` bots may touch.

`argocd/manifests/talos/repos.json` is the single source of truth: it drives
the forge collaborator grants (this script), the talos pod's clone loop
(delivered there as a kustomize-generated ConfigMap whose hashed name rolls
the pod on every policy merge), and — since the issue-driven work cycles —
the per-repo talos webhook (this script again). Before it existed those were
independent manual steps, and a missing grant was invisible — Forgejo answers
404, not 403, for a private repo the caller cannot see, so a repo the bot lacked
access to looked exactly like a typo.

The webhook half: every repo in the pool (pool != "none") gets the forge →
talos hook (the `issues` umbrella event — issue creation, assign, label,
milestone and comment deliveries — the PR-review events, and the `pull_request`
open/close/merge/reopen deliveries), so "share a repo with the agent" also
wires up its triggers. The `pull_request` deliveries exist so talos can
invalidate its issues queue when a PR merges — a merge changes the issue list,
so keeping the queue fresh matters even though a `pull_request` delivery never
spawns a PR session, and today spawns no cycle at all; eblume/talos#151 adds
the issue-side merge edge. The hook is identified by its delivery URL; events
and active-state are reconciled in place (the shared signing secret is only
needed at creation — resolved from $TALOS_FORGE_WEBHOOK_SECRET or 1Password,
so a CI apply that must CREATE a hook fails loudly and asks for a local run
from gilbert). Every repo with
release_hook: true in repos.json additionally gets one forge → horkos release
hook (push + create + pull_request + the terminal action_run_* trio),
identified by its own delivery URL and sharing one signing secret — the
'horkos forge webhook' blumeops-vault item — resolved from
$HORKOS_FORGE_HOOK_SECRET or 1Password, also needed only at creation.

Every hook mutation is read back and checked, because Forgejo's hook edit
API can answer 200 without applying: v16.0.2's editHook maps every event
except action_run_success/failure/recover (its create path and web form do),
so a PATCH adding them is a silent no-op. When the read-back disagrees, the
hook is recreated through the create API — which needs the signing secret,
so in CI that case fails loudly and asks for a local run, like a create.

The label half: every pool repo also gets the `agents` engagement label
(named after the collaborator — talos' FORGE_BOT_LABEL defaults to the bot
login). It is joined by the `no-agents` suppression label: while present on
an issue, talos spawns no issue cycles on it (checked on every delivery, so
removing it re-engages). A third label, `needs-proper-review`
(eblume/talos#158), flags an issue or PR that needs a dedicated human
review; talos clears it when a review lands. The `agents` label is the
secondary engagement path: an
issue opened by an allowlisted creator (Erich — talos'
ISSUE_CREATOR_ALLOWLIST — or the bot itself, for cron-filed briefs) starts a
cycle on creation with no label or assignment, and a comment by anyone but
the bot re-triggers an engaged or allowlisted-created issue. So the label is
for issues not opened by an allowlisted creator and for re-engaging — and on
the read-only repos (blumeops, agents, horkos, talos) it is the only UI
engagement edge, because Forgejo's assignee dropdown only offers write
collaborators and assignment to the bot works only where the bot has write.
A hook without the label is a trap: the delivery pipeline works but no
click in the UI can fire it for anyone but an allowlisted creator (how
horkos#4 sat inert, 2026-08-25). Labels are create-if-missing, matched by
name, and never deleted: deleting a label strips it from every issue
including closed ones (history damage), and a stray label on an off-pool
repo is inert without a hook — so this one part of the reconcile is
deliberately not authoritative.

Labels sit in Forgejo's *issue* scope category, which the narrow CI PAT
(write:repository,read:user) does not carry — it 403s even on label READS
(verified 2026-08-25). So the label half tries the primary token first (in
case the PAT is ever broadened) and falls back to $FORGE_ISSUE_TOKEN or the
blumeops-vault api-token via `op` (the same item mise-tasks/forge-api uses).
In CI neither fallback exists, so the label half is SKIPPED with a loud
yellow warning rather than failing --check forever — label drift is caught
by local runs from gilbert, like hook creation.

RECONCILE IS AUTHORITATIVE. A repo absent from repos.json (or listed with
access "none") has its `agents` collaboration REMOVED, and a talos hook on a
repo outside the pool is removed too — a horkos release hook on a repo
without release_hook: true, likewise. A repo without `horkos_forge: true` —
absent from the list or unflagged — has its `horkos-forge` collaboration
removed the same way; the two bots' grants are reconciled in the same sweep
but against independent policies. Run --dry-run first.

Four repos are pinned read-only by an invariant this script will not let
repos.json override — see PINNED_READ_ONLY. The fence bounds the `agents`
bot's desired access only; the `horkos-forge` bot is deliberately exempt
(write on blumeops is its whole point — see HORKOS_FORGE_USER). It also
refuses write on any repo that carries Actions secrets: write on a repo is
read of its secrets, since every run executes at the pushed ref and is
handed them.

The token is resolved:
  1. --token flag (explicit)
  2. $FORGE_REPO_WRITE_TOKEN (for CI) — and ONLY that variable, see resolve_token
  3. 1Password: op read (for local use on gilbert, prompts biometric)
Its *user* must hold admin on the target repos (collaborator management is
repo-admin-level; eblume owns them all), but the token *scope* only needs
`write:repository` — verified empirically 2026-08-22, retiring the older note
here that claimed such a token 403s. That is the whole point of the CI secret:
a narrowly-scoped eblume PAT (write:repository,read:user — the read:user half is for this script's site-admin check on /users/horkos-forge) instead of the all-scopes admin PAT. A token
that can't read collaborators on a *listed* repo is fatal rather than skipped —
see `blocked` — because every call degrades quietly under the wrong credential,
and a partial apply that reports success is worse than a clean failure.

The branch-protection half reconciles forge/branch-protections.json — the
rules guarding `main` are the outer edge of the agent boundary (who may
push and merge, which checks gate a PR), and before this half they existed
only as UI state. Each declared rule must name every field in
PROTECTION_FIELDS, so the file is the whole rule, not a partial overlay;
list fields compare as sets. A declared rule that is missing is created,
a drifted one is PATCHed with the differing fields and then read back in
full (the hook half's lesson: a 200 is not proof). A rule that exists on
the forge but not in the file is reported and left alone — deleting a
branch protection is not something a reconcile should do quietly. Like
PINNED_READ_ONLY, one fence lives in code: no rule may whitelist either
bot to push, merge or approve (BOT_WHITELIST_FENCE).

Branch protection is APPLIED ONLY FROM A HUMAN RUN. Reads work under the
CI token, so --check and --dry-run report protection drift anywhere; but an
apply authenticated by $FORGE_REPO_WRITE_TOKEN (the CI path) refuses the
protection half loudly after the other halves apply, like a hook create
without its secret. This is policy, not a capability fence — the CI PAT can
edit protections — so that a change to what gates `main` is applied by a
person who saw the diff, not by the merge it gates.

The secret is `FORGE_…`, not `FORGEJO_…`: Forgejo reserves the `FORGEJO_`
prefix for the Actions tokens it injects itself, and rejects creation of a
secret using it with HTTP 400.

Usage:
    mise run forge-reconcile --dry-run    # preview (do this first)
    mise run forge-reconcile              # apply
    mise run forge-reconcile --check      # CI: exit 1 on drift
"""

